    <ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available.&lt;br/&gt;On June 16 2010, the Minister of National Defence introduced Bill C-41, Strengthening Military Justice in the Defence of Canada Act, in the House of Commons and it was given first reading.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The bill amends the National Defence Act to strengthen military justice following the 2003 report of the former Chief Justice of the Supreme Court, the Right Honourable Antonio Lamer, and the May 2009 report of the Standing Senate Committee on Legal and Constitutional Affairs. Among other things it:&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for security of tenure for military judges until their retirement and permits the appointment of part-time military judges.&lt;br/&gt;•	Specifies the purposes, objectives and principles of the sentencing process.&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for additional sentencing options, including absolute discharges, intermittent sentences and restitution.&lt;br/&gt;•	Modifies the composition of a court martial panel according to the rank of the accused person.&lt;br/&gt;•	Modifies the limitation period applicable to summary trials and allows an accused person to waive the limitation periods.&lt;br/&gt;•	Sets out the Canadian Forces Provost Marshal's duties and functions and clarifies his or her responsibilities. It also changes the name of the Canadian Forces Grievance Board to the Military Grievances External Review Committee.&lt;br/&gt;•	Makes amendments to the delegation of the Chief of the Defence Staff's powers as the final authority in the grievance process.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>
    <ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>&lt;div&gt;Le Service d'information et de recherche parlementaires de la Bibliothèque du Parlement rédige actuellement le résumé législatif de ce projet de loi. Entre-temps, le Service met à votre disposition le sommaire suivant.&lt;br/&gt;Le 16 juin 2010, le ministre de la Défense nationale a introduit le projet de loi C-41, Loi visant à renforcer la justice militaire pour la défense du Canada, à la Chambre des communes en première lecture.&lt;br/&gt;Le projet de loi modifie la Loi sur la défense nationale et vise à renforcer la justice militaire suite au rapport de 2003 de l'ancien juge en chef de la Cour suprême le très honorable Antonio Lamer et du rapport de mai 2009 du Comité sénatorial permanent des affaires juridiques et constitutionnelles. Il a notamment pour effet :&lt;br/&gt;•	De prévoir l'inamovibilité des juges militaires jusqu'à l'âge de la retraite et de prévoir leur nomination à temps partiel.&lt;br/&gt;•	D'énoncer les objectifs et principes de la détermination de la peine.&lt;br/&gt;•	De prévoir certaines nouvelles peines, dont l'absolution inconditionnelle, la peine discontinue et le dédommagement.&lt;br/&gt;•	De modifier selon le grade de l'accusé la composition du comité de la cour martiale&lt;br/&gt;•	De modifier la prescription applicable aux procès sommaires et de prévoir la possibilité de s'y soustraire à la demande d'un accusé.&lt;br/&gt;•	D'énoncer les attributions du grand prévôt des Forces canadiennes et de préciser ses responsabilités. Il remplace également le nom du Comité des griefs des Forces canadiennes par celui de « Comité externe d'examen des griefs militaires ».&lt;br/&gt;•	De préciser le pouvoir de délégation du chef d'état-major de la défense en tant qu'autorité de dernière instance dans le processus de traitement des griefs.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>
    <ShortLegislativeSummary>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available.&lt;br/&gt;On June 16 2010, the Minister of National Defence introduced Bill C-41, Strengthening Military Justice in the Defence of Canada Act, in the House of Commons and it was given first reading.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The bill amends the National Defence Act to strengthen military justice following the 2003 report of the former Chief Justice of the Supreme Court, the Right Honourable Antonio Lamer, and the May 2009 report of the Standing Senate Committee on Legal and Constitutional Affairs. Among other things it:&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for security of tenure for military judges until their retirement and permits the appointment of part-time military judges.&lt;br/&gt;•	Specifies the purposes, objectives and principles of the sentencing process.&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for additional sentencing options, including absolute discharges, intermittent sentences and restitution.&lt;br/&gt;•	Modifies the composition of a court martial panel according to the rank of the accused person.&lt;br/&gt;•	Modifies the limitation period applicable to summary trials and allows an accused person to waive the limitation periods.&lt;br/&gt;•	Sets out the Canadian Forces Provost Marshal's duties and functions and clarifies his or her responsibilities. It also changes the name of the Canadian Forces Grievance Board to the Military Grievances External Review Committee.&lt;br/&gt;•	Makes amendments to the delegation of the Chief of the Defence Staff's powers as the final authority in the grievance process.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummary>
